/*
    Upgrade Tiers - raise each item to the highest tier it actually supports

    In the base game a store rerolls the tiered variants of what it stocks each
    time it is reopened, using the buyer's level, and the result may go up, go
    down, or stay the same. This does the same for items already in the party's
    possession, but keeps the result only when the tier rises.

    Which tiers an item supports
    ---------------------------
    Each item carries a MaterialProgress row, and the material table (199) holds that
    row's Material1 to Material15 columns. The row is the authority on what the item
    may become: a Darkspawn Dagger's row stops at Steel, so nothing will ever make it
    Dragonbone. A hardcoded list of materials ignored the row and produced items at
    tiers that do not exist for them.

    The target is the best the vendor could stock for that item at the buyer's level.
    The row's columns are slots, not tiers. What a player calls a tier is the material's
    own rank: Iron/Grey Iron/Steel/Veridium/Red Steel/Silverite/Dragonbone for metal,
    Elm/Ash/Yew/Whitewood/Ironbark/Sylvanwood/Dragonthorn for wood, and the hide ladder
    for leather. The material types table (89) does not carry that rank for wood or
    leather, so the ranks are stated here as per family ladders of material ids,
    checked against all 1016 item templates.

    The target is the best tier the store would stock for a buyer of this level:

        level  1-2  ->  2      level  9-10 ->  6
        level  3-4  ->  3      level 11-13 ->  7
        level  5-6  ->  4      level 14-16 ->  8
        level  7-8  ->  5      level 17+   ->  9

    Tier 7 at level 12 matches what a store was seen offering; the other bands are
    fitted rather than confirmed, so they are worth checking at a second level. The
    whole row is then scanned and the best material whose tier is at or below that
    target is applied.

    A row is a family, not an item. Of the 1016 item templates only 54 distinct rows
    are in use, and one row alone covers 258 templates; just 12 rows belong to a single
    item. So a row states what a *family* scales through, not that each member exists at
    every step. That is why the Darkspawn Dagger stopping at Steel is correct - its whole
    family ends there - and why a generous family row must not be applied to a named
    piece.

    Named items are skipped: they are hand placed rather than generated by a store, so
    the game never rerolls their material. The Helm of Honnleath belongs to a family
    whose row reaches Red Steel while the helm itself was authored in Grey Iron, and
    following the row put it at a tier it was never made in.

    Items worth nothing are skipped, and so are items the game cannot name. A creature's
    built-in attack has a real progression row and can sit in the party inventory - the
    Mabari's bite is one - and raising its tier makes a companion hit harder than the
    designers authored, with nothing in the UI to show it. That item's name reference
    resolves to no string at all, which is why it once printed as a bare "2->7".

    Runes
    -----
    An item's runes are dropped when its material changes. The engine does expose
    AddItemSubItem (822) and RemoveItemSubItem (823), but neither is declared in
    script.ldf and the compiler refuses to bind to an id it cannot resolve, so
    restoring them is out of reach from a script. The slot capability variable that
    other upgrade mods overwrite (ITEM_RUNE_ENABLED) is never written here.

    Reading the result
    ------------------
    Floaties are the only channel that renders in the retail build; the on-screen
    debug text and the GUI log window both produce nothing. The whole run is collected
    and shown once, as a single floaty listing every item that changed, one line each,
    as the tier it was on and the tier it moved to:

        Helm of Honnleath 2->4
        Iron Longsword 4->7
        Dalish Bow 5->7

    Nothing else is printed. A run with no changes says "nothing to upgrade", because
    silence is otherwise indistinguishable from a script that never ran. An item whose
    name is missing shows its template tag instead, so no line loses its label.

    Usage
    -----
        runscript upgrade_tiers          the tier your level earns
        runscript upgrade_tiers 9        force a target tier (1-9)
        runscript upgrade_tiers -9       the same; the sign is ignored

    A single run covers the whole inventory. The engine's own per-script instruction
    limit used to stop this script partway, silently and with no error, which is what
    made earlier versions appear to do nothing; it has far more than enough headroom
    now, so the work no longer has to be spread over repeated invocations.

        Upgraded 24 items to tier 7
        All done

    Only the Warden's inventory is swept. In Dragon Age that inventory is the shared
    party stash, so it holds nearly everything the party owns; the companions' own
    inventories and camp storage hold only spares.
*/

#include "log_h"
#include "utility_h"
#include "wrappers_h"
#include "events_h"
#include "2da_constants_h"


// The engine used to stop any script at 131072 VM instructions, silently, which is
// why this script once had to change only a handful of items per run and be
// re-invoked. DAFix raises that cap, so a single run now covers the whole inventory
// and this is only a safety valve against a runaway loop. It is far above any
// inventory this game can produce.
const int MAX_UPDATES = 10000;

const string BUILD = "ut64";

int g_nChanged = 0;
int g_nStashSize = -1;

// The run is collected here and shown once at the end: the display holds only a
// handful of floaties, so a message per item would bury itself.
string g_sUpdated = "";

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- progression lookups

// The engine knows which materials an item may become: each item carries a
// MaterialProgress row, and the material table (199) holds that row's Material1 to
// Material15 columns. Reading the row respects an item's own ceiling, so a Darkspawn
// Dagger that stops at Steel stays at Steel.
//
// The row alone is not enough. Tier numbers cannot be read from the material types
// table (89) for every family: wood and leather materials return nothing there, which
// silently skipped every bow, crossbow and piece of light armour. The ladders below are
// the material ids per family, checked against all 1016 item templates; no material
// appears in two ladders. They name the same tiers the wiki does, so Dragonbone,
// Dragonthorn and Drakeskin are all tier 7.
const int MAT_COLUMNS = 15;

// Material tiers run 1 to 9. The argument to the command is a tier, not a column.
const int MAX_TIER = 9;

int MaterialAtColumn(int nProgression, int nColumn)
{
    if (nProgression <= 0 || nColumn < 1 || nColumn > MAT_COLUMNS)
    {
        return 0;
    }
    return GetM2DAInt(TABLE_MATERIAL, "Material" + ToString(nColumn), nProgression);
}


// The rank of a material, 1 to 9, across every family. The material types table (89)
// does not carry this for wood or leather, so the ranks are stated here: Iron and Elm
// and Rough Hide are all tier 1, and Dragonbone and Dragonthorn and Drakeskin are all
// tier 7. This is used only to read a tier; the progression row still decides which
// materials an item may become.
int TierOfMaterial(int nMaterial)
{
    switch (nMaterial)
    {
        case 40: case 1: case 60: case 47: case 33: case 79:
            return 1;
        case 41: case 2: case 28: case 48: case 34: case 9:
            return 2;
        case 43: case 8: case 29: case 49: case 35: case 11:
            return 3;
        case 42: case 4: case 30: case 81: case 36: case 15:
            return 4;
        case 44: case 5: case 31: case 50: case 37: case 13:
            return 5;
        case 45: case 3: case 17: case 51: case 38: case 7:
            return 6;
        case 46: case 6: case 32: case 52: case 39: case 10:
            return 7;

        case 20050: case 20010: case 20030: case 20060: case 20040: case 20020:
            return 8;
        case 20051: case 20011: case 20031: case 20061: case 20041: case 20021:
            return 9;
    }
    return 0;
}

// The tier the store stocks for a buyer of this level. Calibrated from observed
// vendor behaviour: at level 11-12 the store offers Dragonbone, which is tier 7. A
// plain (level/2)+1 gives 6 there, one short, so the bands are stated explicitly.
// The creature level is printed in the diagnostics so this can be checked against the
// vendor in one run.
int TierForLevel(int nLevel)
{
    if (nLevel <= 2) { return 2; }
    if (nLevel <= 4) { return 3; }
    if (nLevel <= 6) { return 4; }
    if (nLevel <= 8) { return 5; }
    if (nLevel <= 10) { return 6; }
    if (nLevel <= 13) { return 7; }
    if (nLevel <= 16) { return 8; }
    return 9;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- item work

int CreatureLevel(object oCreature)
{
    return FloatToInt(GetCreatureProperty(oCreature, PROPERTY_SIMPLE_LEVEL));
}

// The run is collected here rather than shown as it happens: the display keeps just a
// handful of floaties, so a message per item buries itself.
void Record(string sText)
{
    g_sUpdated = g_sUpdated + sText + "\n";
}


// Raises one item to the best material its progression row offers within reach of the
// target tier. Returns 1 when the item changed.
int UpgradeItem(object oItem, int nTargetTier, int nIndex, int nCount)
{
    int nOld = GetItemMaterialType(oItem);

    // A named item is one the game never generates: it sits in a chest or is handed
    // over as a reward, at the material it was authored with, and no store ever rerolls
    // it. Its progression row only describes the family it belongs to - one row can cover
    // hundreds of templates - so following the row there invents tiers the piece was
    // never made in. The Helm of Honnleath is the case that showed this.
    if (GetItemUnique(oItem) == TRUE)
    {
        return 0;
    }

    // A value of zero marks something that is not gear: the Mabari's bite, Shale's
    // fists, a quest trinket. The dog's weapon carries a real progression row and sits
    // in the party inventory, so it was reached and raised to Dragonbone, quietly making
    // a companion's natural attack hit far harder than the designers authored.
    if (GetItemValue(oItem) <= 0)
    {
        return 0;
    }

    // The name is the second handle on the same thing, and the more direct one: the
    // Mabari's bite is the item that once printed a bare "2->7", because its name
    // reference resolves to no string at all. It is also the only signal that is
    // definitely present, since GetItemValue reports a price the template may not
    // actually carry. Real gear always has a name, so an unnamed item is not one the
    // player can use, see, or sell.
    if (GetName(oItem) == "")
    {
        return 0;
    }

    // The item's own progression is the authority on what it may become: no row means
    // there is nothing to walk.
    int nProg = GetItemMaterialProgression(oItem);
    if (nProg <= 0)
    {
        return 0;
    }

    // The tier comes from the family ladders, which cover wood and leather as well as
    // metal; the row above decided which materials are permitted, and this decides how
    // good they are. Both are needed: the row without tiers skipped every bow, and
    // tiers without the row invented materials the item does not exist in.
    int nCurrentTier = TierOfMaterial(nOld);
    if (nCurrentTier <= 0)
    {
        return 0;
    }

    // Scan the whole row and keep the best material whose tier is within reach. The
    // row is the hard ceiling, so an item that stops at Steel stays at Steel however
    // generous the level is; the level only decides how far up the row we may go.
    int nBestTier = 0;
    int nBestMaterial = 0;
    int nCol = 1;
    while (nCol <= MAT_COLUMNS)
    {
        int nCandidate = MaterialAtColumn(nProg, nCol);
        if (nCandidate != 0)
        {
            int nCandidateTier = TierOfMaterial(nCandidate);
            if (nCandidateTier <= nTargetTier && nCandidateTier > nBestTier)
            {
                nBestTier = nCandidateTier;
                nBestMaterial = nCandidate;
            }
        }
        nCol = nCol + 1;
    }

    if (nBestMaterial == 0 || nBestTier <= nCurrentTier)
    {
        return 0;
    }

    if (g_nChanged >= MAX_UPDATES)
    {
        return 0;
    }

    // Apply the chosen material. The tier was already checked against the row and the
    // level, so there is no walk here; a rejected write only leaves the item as it was.
    SetItemMaterialType(oItem, nBestMaterial);
    if (GetItemMaterialType(oItem) == nBestMaterial)
    {
        g_nChanged = g_nChanged + 1;
        Record(GetName(oItem) + " " + IntToString(nCurrentTier)
            + "->" + IntToString(nBestTier));
        return 1;
    }
    SetItemMaterialType(oItem, nOld);
    return 0;
}

// Sweeps one creature's inventory, one item at a time.
//
// The type filter is deliberately left at 0. GetItemsInInventory's third argument
// expects a BASE_ITEM_* value, which is a different enumeration from the ITEM_TYPE_*
// constants returned by GetItemType; passing those was selecting arbitrary base item
// types rather than weapons and armour, which is why whole categories of gear were
// never touched. The material ladder is the real filter: anything whose material has
// no tier is skipped for the cost of one call.
int SweepType(object oCreature, int nTargetTier, int nBaseItemType)
{
    int nChanged = 0;
    object[] aItems = GetItemsInInventory(oCreature, GET_ITEMS_OPTION_ALL,
        nBaseItemType, "", TRUE);
    int nCount = GetArraySize(aItems);
    int nIndex = 0;
    int nRefreshes = 0;
    while (nIndex < nCount)
    {
        if (g_nChanged >= MAX_UPDATES)
        {
            break;
        }
        object oItem = aItems[nIndex];

        // Fetching the list again after every change costs a full inventory read each
        // time, which is far too much at this size. Nothing of the sort has ever been
        // needed: no dead reference has been observed. The list is therefore only
        // re-read if a dead reference actually turns up.
        if (oItem == OBJECT_INVALID)
        {
            nRefreshes = nRefreshes + 1;
            if (nRefreshes > 4)
            {
                break;
            }
            aItems = GetItemsInInventory(oCreature, GET_ITEMS_OPTION_ALL,
                nBaseItemType, "", TRUE);
            nCount = GetArraySize(aItems);
            oItem = aItems[nIndex];
            if (oItem == OBJECT_INVALID)
            {
                nIndex = nIndex + 1;
                continue;
            }
        }

        nIndex = nIndex + 1;
        nChanged = nChanged + UpgradeItem(oItem, nTargetTier, nIndex, nCount);
    }
    return nChanged;
}

int UpgradeCreature(object oCreature, int nTargetTier)
{
    if (oCreature == OBJECT_INVALID)
    {
        return 0;
    }

    object[] aItems = GetItemsInInventory(oCreature, GET_ITEMS_OPTION_ALL, 0, "", TRUE);
    int nSize = GetArraySize(aItems);

    // The party stash is shared, so a party member reports the same inventory the
    // Warden does. Sweeping that once per creature re-examined the whole stash a dozen
    // times over: one run reported thousands of items evaluated against an inventory of
    // several hundred. An inventory exactly the size of the Warden's is the stash
    // again, so it is skipped; a companion's own gear is smaller and is swept normally.
    if (g_nStashSize < 0)
    {
        g_nStashSize = nSize;
    }
    else if (nSize == g_nStashSize)
    {
        return 0;
    }

    return SweepType(oCreature, nTargetTier, 0);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- reporting

// Only floaties render in this build: the on-screen debug text and the GUI log
// window both produce nothing, and the script logging functions have no writable
// category (the executable has no ECLog support at all). Floaties do render after
// an item has been changed -- the existing UpgradeItems mod relies on exactly that
// -- so every change is reported as it happens, one floaty per item. A run that
// stops partway therefore ends on the name of the item it stopped at, and no state
// has to survive between invocations.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- entry point

void main()
{
    object oHero = GetHero();
    if (oHero == OBJECT_INVALID)
    {
        return;
    }

    int nTargetTier = 0;
    string sArgument = GetLocalString(GetModule(), "RUNSCRIPT_VAR");
    int nForced = StringToInt(sArgument);

    // The argument is the target tier itself; a negative one means the same thing, so
    // the "-9" form that used to request verbose output still works.
    if (nForced < 0)
    {
        nForced = 0 - nForced;
    }
    if (nForced >= 1 && nForced <= MAX_TIER)
    {
        nTargetTier = nForced;
    }
    int nHeroLevel = CreatureLevel(oHero);
    if (nTargetTier == 0)
    {
        nTargetTier = TierForLevel(nHeroLevel);
    }

    g_nChanged = 0;
    g_nStashSize = -1;
    g_sUpdated = "";

    // Every inventory is swept: the Warden's, which is the shared party stash and
    // holds nearly everything, then each companion's own, then camp storage.
    UpgradeCreature(oHero, nTargetTier);

    object[] aParty = GetPartyList();
    int nParty = GetArraySize(aParty);
    int nIndex = 0;
    while (nIndex < nParty)
    {
        UpgradeCreature(aParty[nIndex], nTargetTier);
        nIndex = nIndex + 1;
    }

    // Companions left at camp are not in the party list; their kits live here.
    object[] aPool = GetPartyPoolList();
    int nPool = GetArraySize(aPool);
    nIndex = 0;
    while (nIndex < nPool)
    {
        UpgradeCreature(aPool[nIndex], nTargetTier);
        nIndex = nIndex + 1;
    }

    // One floaty: every item that changed, one line each, as "name oldTier->newTier".
    // Nothing else is printed - a run with no changes says so, because silence is
    // otherwise indistinguishable from a script that did not run.
    if (g_sUpdated == "")
    {
        DisplayFloatyMessage(oHero, "nothing to upgrade",
            FLOATY_MESSAGE, 16777215, 10.0);
    }
    else
    {
        DisplayFloatyMessage(oHero, g_sUpdated,
            FLOATY_MESSAGE, 16777215, 30.0);
    }
}
